\name{morie_regression}
\alias{morie_regression}
\title{Linear regression with inference, robust errors and diagnostics}


\value{
\code{morie_ols} returns the coefficients with standard errors, t
statistics and p-values, R^2 and adjusted R^2, the residual standard
error and the overall F test.  The covariance functions return
\code{vcov} with its \code{se}; the diagnostics return the statistic
and, where exact, its p-value.
}
\description{
The regression toolkit: ordinary least squares with the inference a
\code{summary()} prints, White's heteroskedasticity-consistent
standard errors in all four small-sample flavours, Newey-West HAC
errors for serially correlated data, and the Breusch-Pagan,
Durbin-Watson and variance-inflation diagnostics.
}
\details{
Every quantity is verified numerically against the implementation the
field uses -- \code{lm}/\code{summary.lm}, \code{sandwich::vcovHC},
\code{sandwich::NeweyWest}, \code{lmtest::bptest},
\code{lmtest::dwtest} and \code{car::vif} -- so these can be relied on
without depending on those packages.  The same functions exist in the
Python package as \code{morie.fn._regression_core}.

Note \code{sandwich::NeweyWest} selects its bandwidth automatically,
whereas \code{morie_newey_west_vcov} defaults to Newey and West's own
\code{floor(4 (n/100)^(2/9))} rule; pass an explicit \code{lags} to
compare the two directly.
}
\references{
White, H. (1980) A heteroskedasticity-consistent covariance matrix
estimator. \emph{Econometrica} 48, 817-838.

Newey, W. K. & West, K. D. (1987) A simple, positive semi-definite,
heteroskedasticity and autocorrelation consistent covariance matrix.
\emph{Econometrica} 55, 703-708.

Koenker, R. (1981) A note on studentizing a test for
heteroscedasticity. \emph{Journal of Econometrics} 17, 107-112.
}
\keyword{regression}
